From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 29 Apr 1994 14:38:04 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-4-282] PCR: Starr's criticisms

First of all, apologies for the gap of several weeks. The good side of 
this is that I've had a chance to read some of Kelley's book in the interim.

On Mon, 11 Apr 1994, Tim Starr wrote:

> >> Sure: it has it that if the nature of consciousness affects the awareness of
> >> the objects of knowledge in any way, then it can't be authentic knowledge.
> >
> >If that is correct statement of the diaphanous model, then I do not hold
> >the diaphanous model,
> 
> Then why were you objecting to the idea of direct awareness by pointing out
> the fallibility of the nature of our consciousness?

Because I don't think the authenticity of our knowledge depends in any 
way upon the infallibility of our senses. I wasn't objecting to the idea 
of authentic knowledge. I have certainly objected to the idea of "direct 
awareness" and to the idea of infallible knowledge, but those are 
separate issues.

> > and I'm also quite confident that the diaphanous
> >model is not *presupposed* in any of Hume's arguments, so I've completely
> >lost the thread of your argument. 
> 
> Yes, it is, albeit indirectly.  Hume's skepticism rests upon his represen-
> tationalism, which rests upon a rejection of realism which rests upon the
> diaphanous view of conciousness, on the argument that the senses would have
> to be infallible in order for our awareness to be direct; they are fallible,
> so realism is incorrect.

You are paraphrasing Kelley's analysis of Hume. I think that Kelley 
tended to lump Hume in with Kant and Descartes, and mistakenly thought 
that a lot of arguments that are effective against Kant and Descartes 
would be effective against Hume as well. In fact, Hume's arguments 
against induction have nothing to do with whether or not we perceive 
things directly or indirectly; they have to do with the things we 
actually perceive vis a vis the things (causation, inductive laws) that 
we do not perceive at all, no matter how indirectly.

> >> >What exactly do you find problematic
> >> >about Hume's theory of causality, and how do those problems apply to this
> >> >discussion? 
> >> 
> >> The problem with Hume's theory of causality is precisely the one he made
> >> such a big deal out of: if it is accepted, then induction is impossible.
> >
> >Well, since in fact induction (as it was commonly understood before the 
> >twentieth century) is impossible,
> 
> Do you mean for three or four centuries before the 20th century, or all the
> way back to ancient times?  If the latter, I think your claim is false.  I
> need to know the definition of induction you're using in order to discuss
> it any further.

Say going back to Bacon's time and the Novum Organum, if you like. In 
fact, it continues to be the commonly held meaning of "induction" in 
mainstream schools of philosophy today, and leads to such garbage as 
Wittgenstein and Rorty.

> Because every time I try to read Bartley, I get stuck with all sorts of
> questions he doesn't seem to answer, so I was hoping some of you who can
> get through him could explain things to me.  The best I've gotten thus far
> is a brief tour of the failings of Modern epistemology and lots of political
> disanalogies.

I thought Max's posts on the topic were a lot better than mine. What 
questions were unanswered?

> >>>To say that I have no reason to believe it is also incorrect. My claim is
> >>>that I cannot logically deduce it from a principle or set of principles
> >>>that would not themselves be subject to your demand for justification and
> >>>proof. That is not equivalent to a claim that I have no reason to believe
> >>>it. 
> >>
> >>There's some other reason to believe it that you're hiding?
> >
> >Yes, and I haven't been hiding it. I believe it because it's more 
> >reasonable than any of its alternatives.
> 
> You have, too, been hiding it.  This is the first I've read of this new
> criterion of "reasonableness" of yours.  Definition, please?

Criteria of reasonableness: logically consistent, not contradictory of
non-problematic empirical facts, high explanatory power (compared to
alternatives), does not unnecessarily posit additional entities (compared
to alternatives), logically strong, etc. This is certainly not the first
you've heard of the various criteria PCRists use in distinguishing better
theories from worse ones. 

I would provide you with a definition except that PCR recognizes *many*
criteria as rational, and I really don't believe you need to have the
terms above (at least not the first two, which are the most important)
*defined* for you. 

> > You can't refute what you don't understand.
> 
> And that's why.  I've found PCR most evasive.  The only things I've been
> able to pin down about it are truistic, common to many schools of thought.
> As soon as I suggest that its unique features have the consequences I
> think they do, I get nothing but denials from PCRists.

This kind of assertion is unpersuasive when unaccompanied by examples of 
the things you've heard denied.

> No, that's why I don't believe in Berkeley's idealism.  The primacy of exist-
> ence is axiomatic.

Perhaps you can explain to me how a supposition that existence does not
have "primacy" over consciousness contradicts *itself*? Not that I'm
really interested. You beg the question that an existence/consciousness
dichotomy is real and significant. Until you unbeg this more fundamental
question, I cannot interest myself in the question of which half of the
false dichotomy has "primacy". 

