From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Fri, 29 Apr 1994 21:49:22 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-283] Social Engineering and R&D

>From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
>
>...I had a friend who used to blame communism on...   sir Isaac Newton,
>saying that  after Newton proved  to people  they can learn the laws of
>Nature and use them to concur it, they started believing they can learn
>the laws of social development as well, and use the knowledge to remake
>the society.
>   That's a case of physics envy though.

Yes, it is a case of physics envy, but it's also got a lot of truth to
it.  F.A. Hayek traces the early intellectual history of socialism in
"The Counter-Revolution of Science," and argues that such Founders of
socialism as Saint-Simon and Auguste Comte were attempting to come up with
a "social physics" by the same methods of Newtonian physics.  Saint-Simon
seems to have had a great deal of physics envy - and engineering envy, to
boot.  He lived outside L'Ecole Polytechnique, whose professors he invited
to dinner with him, and admired Newton so much he proposed to have the
entire world ruled by a "Council of Newton," which would be a committee
staffed by scientists from each field of what might be called the "exact"
sciences - physics, engineering, math, chemistry, etc.

>People are bad at social engineering.

Maybe.  It's also possible that they've just been hampered by bad theories
and methods.  An anarco-capitalist social engineering of a sort is concie-
vable - "All right, what's the trouble here?  Aha!  A lack of property
rights!  All right, you own this, you own that.  Now deal with each other."

>Religion provides many others. For example, as recently as a few centuries
>ago,  when the power of the church in the social issues was enormous,  and
>much  of the scientific and technological development was conducted in the
>monasteries,  they dropped all that R&D business,  being carried away with
>their own power as landlords.   They stopped  building universities within 
>the monasteries and did not pay much attention to the fact that the science
>slipped from their control. 

Actually, R&D went into decline along with the Roman Empire, well before the
rise of the Christian Church.  The Church preserved some remnants of it,
but most of it got preserved by the Moslems - who also advanced it in some
ways: "Algebra" itself is named for the Moslem who discovered it.

Charlemagne established the first modern university, but its purpose wasn't
experimental research.  Rather, it's function was to collect and transmit
the heritage of Western civilization to the young.  The development of
experimental research was a side-effect of supporting scholars.  Another
factor leading to the rise of modern R&D was the establishment of a research
team by Prince Henry the Navigator of Portugal, in order to develop cartogra-
phy and navigation for Portugal to overtake Spain in the spice trade with
the Indies.  According to Daniel Boorstin, this was the first research
institute.
 
